{"type":"message","id":"abb0ae8f","parentId":"92fd4f92","timestamp":"2026-05-22T07:33:18.426Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_wDXLoMLvK7hUMgfLTp8VaaHw","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"us-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-arma-bill-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US lawmakers revive strategic Bitcoin reserve push with the ARMA bill\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"strategic Bitcoin reserve\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"US lawmakers renew strategic Bitcoin reserve push with ARMA bill\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CryptoSlate\",\n    \"CryptoFox\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\",\n    \"https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\",\n    \"https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Representative Nick Begich introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act (ARMA) to put a US strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law, with Democratic Representative Jared Golden also backing the effort.\",\n    \"The bill would direct the Treasury Department to create a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate digital asset stockpile for non-Bitcoin crypto already held by the government.\",\n    \"ARMA would require Bitcoin in the reserve to be held for at least 20 years, with Cointelegraph reporting the main explicit exception as sales used to reduce the national debt.\",\n    \"CryptoSlate says the US currently holds about 328,372 BTC worth more than $25 billion, while Cointelegraph cites more than $25.5 billion, making the existing stash the core asset base for the proposal.\",\n    \"Cointelegraph reports ARMA also seeks to acquire up to 1 million Bitcoin over five years through budget-neutral strategies designed to avoid taxpayer funding.\",\n    \"The legislation includes quarterly proof-of-reserve disclosures, independent third-party audits, and stronger statutory protections against future administrations simply changing course by executive preference.\",\n    \"The bill also explicitly protects lawful self-custody and digital property rights, signaling that the reserve push is paired with broader pro-crypto policy language rather than only government accumulation.\"\n  ],\n  \"tweet_quotes\": [],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"[Cointelegraph]\\nIn an interview on Sunday, Patrick Witt, of the President\\u2019s Council of Advisors for Digital Assets, referred to ARMA as \\u2018Version 2\\u2019 of the BITCOIN Act and said the White House has spent considerable time examining the legal implications of a Bitcoin reserve. \\u2018It's a breakthrough as far as getting everything in place, legally sound, properly safeguarding the assets.\\u2019 The push for a federal policy comes as the US currently holds 328,372 Bitcoin worth more than $25.5 billion \\u2014 the most of any nation-state \\u2014 but has sold portions of those holdings through court-ordered actions over the years. \\u2018The US is already one of the largest holders of Bitcoin in the world. But Congress has never set a federal policy on what to do with that asset,\\u2019 said US Representative Jared Golden, one of the 16 co-sponsors of the bill. Under ARMA, Bitcoin must be held for a minimum of 20 years unless it is sold to reduce America\\u2019s national debt, which topped $39 trillion on Wednesday. Like the BITCOIN Act, ARMA also seeks to acquire up to 1 million Bitcoin over five years through budget-neutral strategies, meaning it would avoid using taxpayer money. US Representative Mike Carey argued that as digital assets continue to grow in importance, the bill could strengthen America\\u2019s long-term economic position and help keep it \\u2018competitive on the world stage.\\u2019 Strive CEO and chairman Matt Cole said ARMA is the \\u2018single most important crypto legislation\\u2019 that could come out of Washington DC. Quarterly proof of reserve reports and independent third-party audits of the Bitcoin reserve would be published under ARMA, Begich noted. The bill also seeks to protect digital property rights by affirming that the federal government may not impair the right of individuals to own or self-custody digital assets.\\n\\n[Decrypt]\\nIn brief: Reps. Nick Begich (R-AK) and Jared Golden (D-ME) introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act. The bill seeks formally codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law, which would be managed for at least 20 years. Aimed at cementing a core Trump administration promise, the initiative has faced ongoing hurdles. Rep. Nick Begich introduced legislation on Thursday aimed at enshrining a strategic reserve for Bitcoin in federal law, seeking to cement one of President Donald Trump\\u2019s core campaign promises for digital asset holders ahead of the high-stakes U.S. midterm elections. The American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, would direct the Treasury Department to create and maintain a reserve of the digital asset for a minimum of 20 years, while establishing a stockpile for other cryptocurrencies held by the government. The legislation also mandates the consolidation of what is believed to be billions of dollars in digital assets held across federal agencies\\u2014attained through actions like forfeitures and penalties\\u2014while providing transparency through a proof-of-reserve report. As of last month, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin that Trump created via executive order is still a work in progress. At a conference in Las Vegas, Patrick Witt, executive director of the President\\u2019s Council of Advisors for Digital Assets, said the current administration has spent months \\u2018figuring out\\u2019 the legal interpretations associated with the initiative. Joining Begich as the bill\\u2019s primary Democratic co-lead is Rep. Jared Golden. The ARMA Act meanwhile counts 17 original co-sponsors, including Rep. Mike Collins (R-GA), among the first lawmakers to disclose an investment in a meme coin on Capitol Hill. \\u2018Administrations have auctioned [crypto] off or held it in reserve, according to the whims of the executive branch,\\u2019 Golden said in a statement, noting that a stockpile created under the ARMA Act would enjoy the \\u2018weight of law.\\u2019 Although a former administration official had said that the White House was eager to purchase as much Bitcoin as possible in a \\u2018budget-neutral way,\\u2019 Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent later dented enthusiasm by ruling out any agency purchases. The bill is aimed at providing the flexibility needed to broaden America\\u2019s portfolio of reserve assets that effectively serve as insurance policy, while also safeguarding crypto from \\u2018the whims of Congress or future administrations,\\u2019 Begich said in an X post. Critics such as Maxine Waters argued that crypto does not meet the usual standard for strategic reserve assets.\\n\\n[CryptoSlate]\\nUS lawmakers push new Strategic Bitcoin Reserve act to secure $25 billion federal stash. ARMA would force the government to hold its Bitcoin for two decades to build a strategic financial buffer. A renewed push to solidify the United States\\u2019 foothold in the digital asset market emerged Thursday as lawmakers unveiled legislation to establish a formal strategic Bitcoin reserve and consolidate the federal government's multibillion-dollar cryptocurrency stockpile. Spearheaded by Representative Nick Begich, the American Reserve Modernization Act of 2026 mandates the creation of a secure Bitcoin reserve within the US Department of the Treasury, alongside a separate \\u2018Digital Asset Stockpile\\u2019 for federally held, non-Bitcoin cryptocurrencies. The legislation aims to provide a long-term structure to the government\\u2019s existing digital wealth, primarily amassed through law enforcement seizures. According to data from Bitcoin Treasuries, the US currently holds 328,372 Bitcoin, a stash valued at more than $25 billion. Begich said the act positions the United States to lead confidently in the digital age while protecting taxpayer interests, strengthening financial sovereignty, and reinforcing transparency and stewardship. The bill has broad House support, with more than 16 original co-sponsors listed. If enacted, the bill would radically alter how Washington handles digital assets. Rather than periodically auctioning off seized crypto, the Treasury would be forced to consolidate custody and oversight across all federal agencies. Crucially, the legislation requires that any Bitcoin held in the strategic reserve be maintained for at least 20 years, effectively turning the US government into a long-term \\u2018hodler.\\u2019 To address industry demands for transparency, the bill establishes quarterly public proof-of-reserve reports, independent third-party audits, and direct congressional oversight. The legislation also directs a study into budget-neutral acquisition strategies, explicitly exploring ways to expand reserves without tax increases, deficit spending, or adding to the national debt. It also protects self-custody rights, affirming that the federal government may not impair Americans\\u2019 lawful right to own, transfer, or independently secure their digital assets. CryptoSlate frames the measure as part of mounting pro-Bitcoin momentum in Washington and a legislative follow-on to the strategic reserve executive order and earlier Lummis BITCOIN Act proposals.\\n\\n[CryptoFox]\\nUS Representative Nick Begich has introduced the Reserve Modernization Act, a legislative proposal designed to formalize the role of digital assets within the national treasury. Supported by 16 original co-sponsors, the bill seeks to establish a permanent strategic Bitcoin reserve, integrating federally held digital assets into a long-term storage framework. This initiative marks a significant shift in federal fiscal policy, aiming to treat BTC as a cornerstone of the nation's financial security for the coming decades. The proposed act focuses on the long-term preservation of digital assets. Under the terms of the legislation, any Bitcoin held within the strategic reserve would be locked for a minimum period of 20 years. This provision is intended to prevent short-term liquidation and ensure that the asset serves as a generational store of value. The bill also outlines strategies for the integration of existing federally held digital assets, which often originate from law enforcement seizures. Rather than auctioning these assets, the government would transition them into the sovereign reserve. A key component of the Reserve Modernization Act is its focus on budget-neutral acquisition strategies. Lawmakers are exploring methods to expand the Bitcoin holdings without increasing the national deficit or requiring new taxpayer funding. The bill includes legal safeguards to protect the reserve from future liquidation efforts by subsequent administrations, establish clear jurisdictional oversight for management of blockchain-based assets, and codify the reserve into official law, creating a more permanent foundation than executive action alone. CryptoFox concludes that the bill represents a major step toward institutionalizing cryptocurrency at the federal level and that markets will watch it as a signal of the United States\\u2019 long-term stance on digital finance and sovereign asset management.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779435198425}}
{"type":"message","id":"7dfe77b2","parentId":"abb0ae8f","timestamp":"2026-05-22T07:33:18.426Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_CEVLPxxSh257RAPtHOcqUCg7","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"error\",\n  \"tool\": \"read\",\n  \"error\": \"ENOENT: no such file or directory, access '/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-072258/COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md'\"\n}"}],"details":{"status":"error","tool":"read","error":"ENOENT: no such file or directory, access '/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-072258/COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md'"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779435198425}}
{"type":"message","id":"f1d84636","parentId":"7dfe77b2","timestamp":"2026-05-22T07:33:18.426Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_h2ZQjjf6qAkB3qnIScN1iS2n","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"META\n- SEO Title: Strategic Bitcoin reserve gets ARMA reboot\n- Meta Description: ARMA would lock US Bitcoin holdings for 20 years, add audits, and study budget-neutral paths to a larger federal reserve.\n- URL Slug: strategic-bitcoin-reserve-arma\n- Categories: Regulation, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: strategic Bitcoin reserve\n- Secondary Keywords: ARMA bill, US Bitcoin reserve, Bitcoin stockpile, budget-neutral Bitcoin acquisition\n\n---\n\n# Strategic Bitcoin reserve bill targets 1 million BTC\n\nThe strategic Bitcoin reserve is back in front of Congress. [Cointelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans) and [Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law) report that Representative Nick Begich's American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, would move the policy from executive action into statute, with Democrat Jared Golden joining the push.\n\nThat matters because the US already controls about 328,372 BTC from seizures and forfeitures, a stash worth more than $25 billion based on the reporting behind the bill. The debate is no longer about whether Washington can touch Bitcoin. It is about whether Congress wants Treasury to treat those holdings as strategic reserves instead of inventory that may be sold.\n\nFor traders, ARMA matters less as an instant demand story and more as a supply story. A legal framework that limits sales, mandates audits, and studies further accumulation would give the market a clearer way to price federal holdings.\n\n## Why ARMA changes the reserve debate\n\nARMA is designed to solve a basic policy weakness. A reserve created by executive order can be revised or ignored by a later administration. A reserve written into law is harder to unwind and easier for the market to model.\n\nThe bill would direct Treasury to maintain a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate digital asset stockpile for non-Bitcoin crypto already held by the government. That separation is important. It signals that lawmakers want Bitcoin treated as the strategic asset while other seized tokens remain in a different bucket.\n\n### Statute matters more than symbolism\n\nWashington has not handled seized crypto with a single playbook. Some holdings were auctioned. Others sat idle. That patchwork created uncertainty every time wallets moved or headlines hinted at federal sales.\n\nARMA tries to replace that discretionary approach with rules. If Congress defines custody, reporting, and holding periods in law, traders get fewer surprises from policy swings tied to elections or agency preferences.\n\n### The US is starting with a large stash\n\nThis is not a proposal built on hypothetical reserves. The federal government already holds one of the largest sovereign Bitcoin positions in the world, giving the bill immediate market relevance even before any new purchases are approved.\n\nThat existing balance is why ARMA draws attention beyond politics. If the government stops treating seized BTC as a source of periodic supply, then part of the overhang that traders watch today starts to look more like dormant reserve inventory.\n\n## What ARMA could change for Bitcoin supply\n\nThe biggest market effect would come from restricting sales. Coverage of the bill says Bitcoin in the reserve would need to be held for at least 20 years, with the main explicit exception tied to reducing the national debt.\n\nThat kind of lockup would not make coins disappear, but it would change how investors think about federal ownership. Instead of assuming seized BTC might return to market on a policy shift, traders would start valuing that stash as long-duration supply sitting outside normal circulation.\n\n### A 20-year hold would reset overhang assumptions\n\nLong holding periods matter because federal transfers have repeatedly created noise around Bitcoin price action. Even when sales were limited, the possibility of liquidation forced traders to price in event risk.\n\nARMA aims to reduce that uncertainty. If Treasury is required to hold reserve Bitcoin for two decades, then future wallet movements become less threatening unless Congress changes the rules or debt-reduction sales come into view.\n\n### Budget-neutral buying is the upside lever\n\nThe proposal also keeps alive a target of acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years through budget-neutral strategies. That is a notable headline, but it should not be read as an approved buying program.\n\nThe key phrase is budget-neutral. Supporters are trying to preserve room for expansion without new taxpayer funding, tax increases, or higher deficits. Until lawmakers explain the mechanism, traders should treat this as optional future upside, not near-term spot demand.\n\n## What traders should watch next\n\nARMA has bipartisan support, which gives it more credibility than a purely partisan messaging bill. Still, co-sponsors do not guarantee passage, and the reserve idea still faces skeptics who question whether Bitcoin belongs beside traditional strategic assets.\n\nThat means the next phase is procedural. Traders should focus less on political slogans and more on whether the bill starts moving through committees, picks up broader leadership support, and survives debate over how much flexibility Treasury should have.\n\n### Audits could remove a recurring blind spot\n\nARMA includes quarterly proof-of-reserve disclosures, independent third-party audits, and congressional oversight. Those features may sound administrative, but they matter for price discovery.\n\nBetter transparency would help confirm the size and status of federal Bitcoin holdings. It could also reduce rumor-driven volatility when government wallets move, because traders would have more official information about what the reserve actually contains and how it is managed.\n\n### Self-custody language broadens the bill's signal\n\nThe bill also protects lawful self-custody and digital property rights. That widens the proposal beyond simple reserve management and frames it as part of a broader pro-crypto policy stance.\n\nFor the market, that broader framing cuts two ways. It may help the bill attract industry support, but it can also invite a wider policy fight that slows passage. Traders should keep both possibilities in mind.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe strategic Bitcoin reserve debate is moving from headline politics to legislative design. ARMA would formalize a US Bitcoin reserve, separate other federally held crypto into a stockpile, require a 20-year hold, and add audit rules that make government custody easier to track. For traders, the core issue is simple: if Congress turns federal BTC into strategic inventory instead of potential sell-side supply, the market's long-term supply picture changes.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. What is the ARMA bill trying to do?**\n\nARMA would direct the Treasury Department to create a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate stockpile for other federally held digital assets. The goal is to put the strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law instead of leaving it vulnerable to executive changes. That would give the policy more permanence and clearer operating rules.\n\n**2. How much Bitcoin does the US already hold?**\n\nCoverage cited in the bill reports puts current federal holdings at about 328,372 BTC, worth more than $25 billion at recent prices. Most of that came from seizures and forfeitures rather than direct market purchases. That makes the reserve debate relevant immediately, not just in a future accumulation scenario.\n\n**3. Would ARMA force the government to hold Bitcoin?**\n\nYes, the reported framework requires Bitcoin in the reserve to be held for at least 20 years. The main explicit exception mentioned in coverage is selling to reduce the national debt. If that language survives, it would sharply limit expectations for routine federal sales.\n\n**4. Does this mean the US will buy 1 million BTC soon?**\n\nNot yet. The bill preserves a goal of acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years through budget-neutral strategies, but that is not the same as an active purchase schedule. Traders should separate long-range authorization from near-term execution.\n\n**Sources:**\n- Cointelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans\n- Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\n- CryptoSlate: https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\n- CryptoFox: https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\n\n[Word Count: 1169]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779435198426}}
