{"type":"message","id":"22ab29f8","parentId":"66e3f56a","timestamp":"2026-05-22T07:33:30.251Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_eyJodtXE9fc9bgLIYAyEaefv","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-writer/COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":10633,"output":303,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":10936,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779435203737,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiEflW5dsCeCexWNVCel2QU8bvo5p"}}
{"type":"message","id":"694aa9bc","parentId":"22ab29f8","timestamp":"2026-05-22T07:33:30.287Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_eyJodtXE9fc9bgLIYAyEaefv","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL RULES\n\nFollow these rules for every article. Interior structure (how many sections, headings, hook style) is your editorial decision within the borders below.\n\n---\n\n## Hard rules (never break)\n\n**Content quality**\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use `—`. Use a comma, period, or restructure.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype or fluff (\"amazing\", \"shocking\").\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only, inside H3 sections if you use them. Never in Conclusion or opening hook.\n\n**SEO and links**\n- **ANCHOR LINKS:** Exactly **2** markdown links to URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON.source_urls` (distinct URLs, no repeats). Both in the **opening hook or first H2 only**.\n- **TWEET QUOTES (optional):** If `tweet_quotes` has `verified: true`, weave **one** short quote in hook or first H2. Max **1** tweet link (separate from the 2 source anchors).\n- **KEYWORDS:** Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words); in first 100 words; in at least one H2 and in Conclusion. Use secondary keywords in H2/H3 where natural.\n\n**Length**\n- **Word count:** Body **1000–1200** words (aim **1100**). Count body only; META, Sources, and `[Word Count:]` line are not counted.\n\n**Fixed section order**\n```\nMETA → H1 (hook) → H2 body sections → ## Conclusion → ## FAQs → **Sources:** → [Word Count: NNNN]\n```\n- **## Conclusion** must appear immediately before **## FAQs**.\n- **## FAQs** must be the **last** section in the content body (immediately before **Sources:**).\n- Do not put FAQs before Conclusion or interleave FAQs in the body.\n\n---\n\n## Structure borders (you decide inside these limits)\n\n| Element | Minimum | Maximum |\n|---------|---------|---------|\n| H2 body sections (excluding Conclusion, FAQs) | 2 | 4 |\n| H3 subsections (`###`) total | 3 | 6 |\n| FAQ items under `## FAQs` | 3 | 6 |\n\nWithin these borders, you choose:\n- How many H2s, H3s, and FAQs to use\n- Heading text and angle for each section\n- How to open the article (2–4 paragraphs under H1)\n- Section depth, bullet lists, and flow\n\nThere is **no** prescribed mapping from fact count to section count. Decide what structure best serves **this** story.\n\nEach H3 should draw on distinct material from research; do not pad with empty sections.\n\n---\n\n## META block (required first)\n\nThe first word of the file must be **META**.\n\nCount characters in `<thinking>` before writing:\n\n| Field | Your max | Rank Math cap |\n|-------|----------|---------------|\n| SEO Title | **55** | 60 |\n| Meta Description | **155** | 160 |\n| URL Slug | **70** | 75 |\n\n```\nMETA\n- SEO Title: [primary keyword early; add | Coinography only if ≤55 chars total]\n- Meta Description: [hook + detail; ≤155 chars]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase hyphens; 3–5 words; ≤70 chars]\n- Categories: [e.g. Regulation, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [...]\n- Secondary Keywords: [...]\n```\n\n---\n\n## H1 and body\n\nAfter META and `---`, write **one** H1:\n\n`# [Primary keyword in first 5 words; include a price or number when relevant]`\n\nThen your hook and H2/H3 body sections. Heading names and section count are your choice within the borders above.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion (required)\n\n`## Conclusion`\n\nRestate the event with primary keyword and actionable close for traders. Length is your call within the total word band.\n\n---\n\n## FAQs (required, last before Sources)\n\n`## FAQs`\n\nWrite questions that fit **this** story (no fixed question bank). **3–6** items within the FAQ border.\n\nFormat each item:\n```\n**1. Your question here?**\n\nAnswer paragraph, 3–4 sentences.\n```\n\nRenumber 1..N. Do not use markdown list syntax for questions.\n\n---\n\n## Sources footer (required)\n\nDo not use `## Sources` or `## Word Count` as headings.\n\n```\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication]: [URL]\n- [Publication]: [URL]\n\n[Word Count: 1100]\n```\n\nFinal line: `[Word Count: NNNN]` with digits only.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-flight (in `<thinking>`)\n\n- [ ] META char counts within limits\n- [ ] 2 source URLs planned for hook/first H2\n- [ ] Structure choices within H2/H3/FAQ borders\n- [ ] Body word budget 1000–1200\n- [ ] Order: Conclusion then FAQs then Sources\n\n## Post-output check\n\n- [ ] 1 H1; H2/H3/FAQ counts within borders\n- [ ] Conclusion before FAQs; FAQs before Sources\n- [ ] 1000–1200 body words\n- [ ] Exactly 2 source links in hook/first H2\n- [ ] No em-dashes or banned phrases\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779435210287}}
{"type":"message","id":"790b7198","parentId":"694aa9bc","timestamp":"2026-05-22T07:34:02.203Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_XoIAn0rLdPznyjWa1NkcoFhA","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-072258/article/raw.md","content":"META\n- SEO Title: Strategic Bitcoin reserve gets ARMA reboot\n- Meta Description: ARMA would lock US Bitcoin holdings for 20 years, add audits, and study budget-neutral paths to a larger federal reserve.\n- URL Slug: strategic-bitcoin-reserve-arma\n- Categories: Regulation, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: strategic Bitcoin reserve\n- Secondary Keywords: ARMA bill, US Bitcoin reserve, Bitcoin stockpile, budget-neutral Bitcoin acquisition\n\n---\n\n# Strategic Bitcoin reserve bill targets 1 million BTC\n\nThe strategic Bitcoin reserve is back in Congress. [Cointelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans) and [Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law) report that Representative Nick Begich's American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, would move the policy from executive action into statute, with Democrat Jared Golden backing the effort.\n\nThat shift matters because the US already controls about 328,372 BTC from seizures and forfeitures, a stock worth more than $25 billion in the reporting behind the bill. The question is no longer whether Washington holds Bitcoin. It is whether Treasury should treat that pile as a strategic asset instead of a source of future sales.\n\nFor traders, ARMA is mainly a supply story. If lawmakers restrict sales, require audits, and keep open a path to more accumulation, the market gets a cleaner framework for pricing federal holdings.\n\n## Why the strategic Bitcoin reserve push matters now\n\nARMA tries to fix a basic weakness in current policy. A reserve created by executive order can be narrowed, delayed, or reversed by a future White House. A reserve written into law is harder to unwind and easier for markets to model.\n\nThe bill would direct Treasury to maintain a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate digital asset stockpile for non-Bitcoin crypto already held by the government. That split is important because it suggests lawmakers want Bitcoin treated as the strategic asset, while other tokens remain in a different policy bucket.\n\n### Congress wants rules, not wallet-by-wallet improvisation\n\nFederal agencies have never followed one clear script for seized crypto. Some assets were sold, some were held, and some became headline risk whenever government-linked wallets moved onchain.\n\nARMA aims to replace that uncertainty with statute. If custody, reporting, and holding periods are defined in law, traders face less event risk tied to elections, agency discretion, or sudden liquidation fears.\n\n### The reserve would start with a real balance, not a theory\n\nThis proposal is not starting from zero. The US already holds one of the largest sovereign Bitcoin positions in the world, so the bill matters even before any new buying authority is clarified.\n\nThat existing stash changes the debate. If Washington stops treating seized BTC as sell-side inventory, part of the overhang hanging over the market starts to look more like dormant reserve supply.\n\n## How ARMA could reshape Bitcoin supply expectations\n\nThe biggest market effect would come from limiting sales. Reporting on the bill says Bitcoin in the reserve would need to be held for at least 20 years, with the clearest explicit exception tied to reducing the national debt.\n\nThat would not remove coins from existence, but it would change how investors classify them. Instead of viewing federal BTC as potential periodic supply, traders could begin treating it as long-duration inventory parked outside normal circulation.\n\n### A 20-year hold could cut a persistent overhang\n\nGovernment transfers have often created noise around Bitcoin price action. Even when actual sales were limited, the possibility of liquidation forced markets to price in policy risk.\n\nA mandatory hold period would dampen that threat. If Treasury must keep reserve Bitcoin for two decades, routine wallet movements become less alarming unless Congress later changes the rules or debt-reduction sales enter the picture.\n\n### Budget-neutral buying is the upside scenario\n\nARMA also preserves the idea of acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years through budget-neutral strategies. That is a large headline number, but it should not be confused with an approved purchase plan.\n\nThe key phrase is budget-neutral. Supporters want room to expand reserves without new taxpayer funding, higher deficits, or direct pressure on the federal budget. Until lawmakers explain the mechanism, traders should treat this as optional upside, not near-term spot demand.\n\n## What traders should watch if the bill advances\n\nBipartisan support gives ARMA more weight than a messaging bill, but sponsorship alone does not mean passage. The reserve idea still faces critics who argue Bitcoin does not fit the standard definition of a strategic reserve asset.\n\nThat means process matters more than rhetoric from here. Traders should watch committee movement, leadership backing, and whether lawmakers preserve the strict hold language that gives the proposal most of its supply impact.\n\n### Audits and proof-of-reserve reports matter for price discovery\n\nARMA includes quarterly proof-of-reserve disclosures, independent third-party audits, and congressional oversight. Those features may sound technical, but they address a recurring blind spot in the market.\n\nBetter reporting would help verify the size and status of federal Bitcoin holdings. It could also reduce rumor-driven volatility because traders would have more official information when wallets move or reserve policy comes into question.\n\n### Self-custody language broadens the policy signal\n\nThe bill also protects lawful self-custody and digital property rights. That widens the proposal beyond reserve management and frames it as part of a broader crypto policy push.\n\nThat broader scope could help the bill win industry support, but it could also draw a wider political fight. For traders, that means the reserve thesis is strong enough to watch closely, while the legislative path remains uncertain.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe strategic Bitcoin reserve debate is shifting from campaign language to legislative design. ARMA would formalize a US Bitcoin reserve, separate other federally held crypto into a stockpile, require a 20-year hold, and add audits that make government custody easier to track. If Congress turns federal BTC into strategic inventory instead of potential sell-side supply, traders may need to reprice a meaningful part of Bitcoin's long-term supply picture.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. What would the ARMA bill do?**\n\nARMA would direct the Treasury Department to create a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate stockpile for other federally held digital assets. The goal is to put the strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law instead of leaving it dependent on executive action. That would give the policy more permanence and clearer operating rules.\n\n**2. How much Bitcoin does the US already hold?**\n\nReporting cited around the bill places current federal holdings at about 328,372 BTC, worth more than $25 billion at recent prices. Most of that came from seizures and forfeitures rather than open-market buying. That is why the proposal matters immediately, even without a new purchase program.\n\n**3. Would ARMA stop the government from selling Bitcoin?**\n\nNot completely, but it would sharply limit flexibility. The reported framework requires Bitcoin in the reserve to be held for at least 20 years, with the main explicit exception tied to reducing the national debt. If that survives the legislative process, expectations for routine federal sales would fall.\n\n**4. Does the bill mean the US will buy 1 million BTC soon?**\n\nNo. The proposal keeps alive a goal of acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years through budget-neutral strategies, but that is not the same as an active purchase schedule. Traders should separate long-range authorization language from near-term execution.\n\n**Sources:**\n- Cointelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans\n- Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\n- CryptoSlate: https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\n- CryptoFox: https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\n\n[Word Count: 1068]"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":11893,"output":2297,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":14190,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779435210288,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiEfrYUHQucpP2FO4YfnycBKWDaKI"}}
